      "title": "More Than 60 Killed in Oromia as Ethiopia’s Instability Deepens, Raising Alarm for Mogadishu - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/more-than-60-killed-in-oromia-as-ethiopias-instability-deepens-raising-alarm-for-mogadishu/",
      "date": "Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:10:52 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Oromia-church-kills.jpg",
      "content": "More Than 60 Killed in Oromia as Ethiopia's Instability Deepens, Raising Alarm for Mogadishu - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Security Nationwide More Than 60 Killed in Oromia as Ethiopia’s Instability Deepens, Raising Alarm for Mogadishu October 27, 2026 6 min read MOGADISHU , The massacre of more than 60 Christians in Ethiopia’s Oromia region is not a distant tragedy for Somalia. It is a direct indicator of the escalating instability in a neighboring state whose internal conflicts are increasingly intertwined with Somalia’s own security and political trajectory. A Pattern of Sectarian Violence According to the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, more than 60 people were killed in attacks on three areas in the Shirka district of Oromia. A local official blamed the Alliance for Survival, a newly formed rebel coalition that includes the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA). The OLA has denied involvement in previous such attacks. Eyewitnesses told BBC Amharic that five of those killed were children, and that 20 houses were burned to the ground. The Ethiopian Human Rights Council reported in July 2026 that 458 people had been killed in attacks in that area over the previous four years, describing them as religiously motivated and targeting followers of the Orthodox Church. This latest massacre is one of the deadliest in that period. The Rebel Alliance Behind the Chaos The Shirka killings are part of a wider pattern of escalating violence across Ethiopia. A coalition of seven armed groups, including the TPLF, the Fano militia in Amhara, and the OLA, has announced the formation of the Ethiopian Peoples’ Forces Alliance for Survival, aimed at removing Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed’s government. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the alliance , the coalition spans half of Ethiopia’s twelve regional states and brings together groups that have previously fought one another, making the alignment an unusual development in the country’s fragmented opposition landscape. The Ogaden National Liberation Front (ONLF), a Somali-majority armed group, is among the signatories. Its inclusion is particularly notable given the group’s 2018 peace agreement with the Ethiopian government and its subsequent focus on diplomatic efforts to advance the interests of ethnic Somalis in Ethiopia’s Somali Regional State. The Moyale Clashes: A Somali Dimension The violence in Oromia directly affects ethnic Somali communities in Ethiopia. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the Moyale clashes , heavy fighting erupted between Oromo and Somali communities in the border town, killing at least nine people and forcing more than 2,500 Ethiopians to flee into Kenya. The trigger was a dispute over the display of the Somali Regional State flag at an administrative office in an area administered by Oromia. These clashes demonstrate how local disputes over flags, administrative boundaries, and political control can escalate into deadly confrontations with regional consequences. For Somalia, the displacement of ethnic Somalis across the border into Kenya carries humanitarian and security implications, particularly in a borderland region where Al-Shabaab has historically exploited instability. What This Means for Somalia: The Security Gap For Somalia, the implications are direct and urgent. Ethiopia’s internal conflicts are already drawing its military resources away from Somalia. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of Ethiopia’s military redeployment , Addis Ababa is planning to withdraw more than 3,000 troops from the Bay and Bakool regions of South West State to redeploy them to Tigray, Amhara, and Afar. This withdrawal would leave a security gap in areas where Ethiopian forces have been fighting Al-Shabaab, and it comes as the AUSSOM mission faces its own funding and mandate uncertainties. The timing is particularly delicate. The UN political mission in Somalia is closing its doors on October 31, ending more than three decades of international political stewardship. The AUSSOM mission faces a funding cliff and a potential withdrawal of Ugandan forces. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the transition , Somalia is entering a phase where it will have fewer international buffers and more direct responsibility for its own security. Ethiopia’s regional position is also hardening. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of Ethiopia’s response to the Alamein Declaration , Addis Ababa has rejected what it describes as “coercive measures” and “acts of destabilization” aimed at constraining its Red Sea ambitions. The four-nation Alamein Declaration, signed by Egypt, Eritrea, Somalia, and Sudan, formalized coordination on regional security and affirmed that Red Sea governance is the responsibility of littoral states. Ethiopia’s exclusion from that framework, combined with its internal conflicts, creates a volatile regional dynamic. Critical Note The massacre in Shirka is a crime that demands accountability, and the Ethiopian government’s failure to protect its citizens, particularly religious minorities, is a damning indictment of its security apparatus. The Orthodox Church’s statement that it had repeatedly raised concerns with authorities but received no solution is a familiar refrain in Ethiopia, where local communities have borne the brunt of conflicts that the state is either unable or unwilling to contain. For Somalia, the lesson is one of exposure. Ethiopia’s internal fragmentation is not a spectator sport. It is a process that is already reshaping the security architecture of the Horn of Africa. The withdrawal of Ethiopian troops from Somalia, the displacement of civilians across the Kenyan border, and the formation of a rebel alliance that includes armed groups from multiple regions all point in the same direction: Ethiopia is entering a period of prolonged instability, and Somalia will feel the consequences whether it is prepared or not. The Somali government’s focus on its own political crisis, the contested electoral process, and the fight against Al-Shabaab is understandable. But it cannot afford to ignore the collapse of order next door. The same international partners who are withdrawing from Somalia are also watching Ethiopia with concern. The same Al-Shabaab that Somalia is fighting has exploited Ethiopian instability in the past. The same borderlands that are now scenes of sectarian violence are the corridors through which weapons, fighters, and displaced people move. The Shirka massacre is a warning. It is not the first, and it will not be the last. Somalia’s security is not confined to its borders. It is shaped by the stability of its neighbors, and Ethiopia’s stability is deteriorating rapidly. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com ONLF Joins Seven-Group Ethiopian Alliance Against Abiy Government Ethiopia’s Troop Withdrawal and the Egypt-Eritrea Alignment: What It Means for Somalia Deadly Oromo-Somali Clashes in Moyale Kill Nine, Displace Thousands to Kenya Ethiopia Rejects ‘Coercive Measures’ as Egypt, Eritrea, Somalia and Sudan Formalize Bloc Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Somali Army Kills 75 Al-Shabaab Militants in Major Operation Near Buurhakaba You may also like Security Somali Army Kills 75 Al-Shabaab Militants in Major Operation Near Buurhakaba October 27, 2026 4 min read Security Heavy Fighting Erupts in Burijeed as Laftagareen Forces Attack Army Positions Near Baidoa October 26, 2026 5 min read Security NISA Arrests Suspect in Hawo Taako Control Post Bombing, Links Him to 10 Other Attacks October 26, 2026 4 min read Latest News Somalia’s 2027 Budget Shrinks by $127 Million, 9.2% as Cabinet Approves Draft The $19 Gap: How Bosasso and Kismayo Are Undercutting Mogadishu Port and Draining Federal Revenue More Than 60 Killed in Oromia as Ethiopia’s Instability Deepens, Raising Alarm for Mogadishu President Mohamud Asks Ugandan Troops to Stay Longer as Museveni Calls for United Front Somali Army Kills 75 Al-Shabaab Militants in Major Operation Near Buurhakaba Information Minister and Somali Envoy in Baku to Cheer U-15 Team at FIFA World Cup UN Closes Political Mission in Somalia After Three Decades Villa Somalia Touts 1.8 Million Voter Registrations as Opposition Rejects Electoral Process Mogadishu Discharges Citizen Linked to Kenyan Ebola Victim After 21-Day Monitoring Justice Minister Defends Cabinet-Parliament Dual Roles, Delays Separation of Powers, Citing Legal Gaps Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "More than 60 people were killed in Ethiopia's Oromia region, highlighting the escalating instability in the country and raising concerns for neighboring Somalia. The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church reported that the victims were targeted in attacks attributed to a newly formed rebel coalition, the Alliance for Survival, which includes the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA). Eyewitness accounts revealed that children were among the dead and numerous homes were destroyed. This incident is part of a broader pattern of sectarian violence in Oromia, where over 458 people have died in similar attacks in recent years. The violence also affects ethnic Somali communities, evidenced by clashes in the border town of Moyale. The ongoing turmoil in Ethiopia is diverting military resources away from Somalia, creating a security gap as the UN political mission concludes. The situation underscores the interconnectedness of regional security, with Ethiopia's instability posing direct threats to Somalia's safety and political landscape.",
