      "content": "Egypt to Deploy Troops in Somalia Under Bilateral Deal Ahead of Aussom Integration - allAfrica.com AllAfrica English (current) En Français Toggle navigation My Account Toggle navigation AllAfrica My Account English (current) En Français Egypt to Deploy Troops in Somalia Under Bilateral Deal Ahead of Aussom Integration 26 October 2026 Addis Standard (Addis Ababa) Addis Abeba — Egypt is preparing to deploy troops to Somalia under a bilateral military cooperation agreement before transferring approximately 1,000 personnel into the African Union Support and Stabilisation Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM), raising concerns over operational coordination and command structures. According to The East African report on 25 October, the initial deployment is expected to comprise a brigade-sized battle group with support elements in Middle Shabelle, north of Mogadishu. A tactical headquarters is planned at Ceelcade, near Ciciley airfield, an area currently held by Burundian AU troops. Additional Egyptian forces could be stationed elsewhere at Somalia's request, although Cairo has not disclosed the full size of its deployment. The African Union has yet to publicly clarify how the bilateral force will coordinate with AUSSOM and other contingents. Burundi's troops, which receive logistical support through the UN Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS), have reportedly not been formally notified of the proposed arrangements and have indicated they will not operate under a bilateral command structure. They are prepared to withdraw once replaced. Egypt was allocated approximately 1,091 troops under AUSSOM in late 2024 but delayed formal participation, citing funding constraints. Deploying under a bilateral agreement before integration raises questions over command authority, accountability, logistics and potential operational overlap. Follow us on WhatsApp | LinkedIn for the latest headlines Egypt and Somalia signed a military cooperation agreement in August 2024, followed by Egyptian shipments of weapons, ammunition and armoured vehicles, as well as military training and advisory support. In August 2025, Somalia's Defence Ministry announced the completion of specialised training for Egyptian units intended for AUSSOM . A 16-member Egyptian military reconnaissance delegation visited Mogadishu the following month to assess deployment sites and logistics. Addis Standard previously reported o n preparations for the deployment , including troop movements and site work at Ceelcade, as formal integration remained delayed by funding and logistical challenges. The planned deployment comes as AUSSOM faces financial and operational pressures while supporting Somali forces against Al-Shabaab. Uncertainty over logistical support through UNSOS, amid questions about continued US funding, has further complicated the mission's sustainability. The move also carries regional implications amid longstanding tensions between Egypt and Ethiopia over the Nile waters and the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD), alongside competing interests in Red Sea access. Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Top Headlines Egypt Ethiopia Arms and Armies Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later. Ethiopia has recently withdrawn some troops from Somalia, while analysts warn that competing security partnerships could complicate operations and draw Somalia's security arrangements into wider regional rivalries. Observers are cautioning against the absence of a clear public framework for coordinating bilateral forces with AUSSOM and its impact about command, accountability and the transition from existing contingents. AS Read the original article on Addis Standard . Tagged: Egypt East Africa Conflict Ethiopia Arms and Armies North Africa Somalia Copyright © 2026 Addis Standard . All rights reserved. Distributed by AllAfrica Global Media ( allAfrica.com ). To contact the copyright holder directly for corrections — or for permission to republish or make other authorized use of this material, click here. AllAfrica publishes around 600 reports a day from more than 90 news organizations and over 500 other institutions and individuals , representing a diversity of positions on every topic. We publish news and views ranging from vigorous opponents of governments to government publications and spokespersons. Publishers named above each report are responsible for their own content, which AllAfrica does not have the legal right to edit or correct. Articles and commentaries that identify allAfrica.com as the publisher are produced or commissioned by AllAfrica . To address comments or complaints, please Contact us . AllAfrica is a voice of, by and about Africa - aggregating, producing and distributing 600 news and information items daily from over 90 African news organizations and our own reporters to an African and global public. We operate from Cape Town, Dakar, Abuja, Johannesburg, Nairobi and Washington DC. Support our work Sign up for our newsletter For Advertisers Subscribe © 2026 AllAfrica Privacy Policy Contact Us About Us AllAfrica - All the Time Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later.",
      "summary": "Egypt is set to deploy troops to Somalia under a bilateral military cooperation agreement, ahead of integrating approximately 1,000 personnel into the African Union Support and Stabilisation Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM). The initial deployment will consist of a brigade-sized battle group stationed in Middle Shabelle, with a tactical headquarters planned at Ceelcade, currently held by Burundian troops. Concerns have arisen regarding operational coordination and command structures, as the African Union has not clarified how the Egyptian forces will integrate with AUSSOM. Additionally, Burundi's troops have not been formally notified of the changes and are prepared to withdraw once replaced. Egypt's military cooperation with Somalia, which began in August 2024, includes weapon shipments and military training. The deployment comes amid financial pressures on AUSSOM and regional tensions, particularly between Egypt and Ethiopia over the Nile waters. Analysts warn that competing security partnerships could complicate Somalia's security arrangements. (159 words)",
