      "title": "Holy Synod Confirms 60 Orthodox Faithful Killed in Latest Attacks in Oromia, Calls for Protection, Justice",
      "link": "https://allafrica.com/stories/202610270080.html",
      "date": "Tue, 27 Oct 2026 04:57:22 GMT",
      "image": "",
      "content": "Ethiopia: Holy Synod Confirms 60 Orthodox Faithful Killed in Latest Attacks in Oromia, Calls for Protection, Justice - allAfrica.com AllAfrica English (current) En Français Toggle navigation My Account Toggle navigation AllAfrica My Account English (current) En Français Ethiopia: Holy Synod Confirms 60 Orthodox Faithful Killed in Latest Attacks in Oromia, Calls for Protection, Justice 26 October 2026 Addis Standard (Addis Ababa) Addis Abeba — The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church's Holy Synod has confirmed that 60 Orthodox faithful were killed in attacks in two districts of Oromia's Arsi Zone on 20 and 24 October, while more than 20 others sustained severe injuries and 20 homes were burned down, according to a statement issued on Monday. The Holy Synod said 14 people were killed in Angada Kebele, specifically in Gora Silingo, Merti Woreda, on 20 October, while another 46 were killed in Tereta, Chole and Birbo in Shirka Woreda on 24 October . \"We have heard with deep sorrow the report submitted by the Diocese Office,\" the Holy Synod said, describing the killings as \"extremely tragic cruelty and suffering inflicted on our citizens.\" The statement, issued on 26 October, said the victims were innocent Orthodox faithful and warned that killings, displacement, physical harm and destruction of property targeting Orthodox Christians in Arsi and other parts of Ethiopia had continued to escalate. Follow us on WhatsApp | LinkedIn for the latest headlines The Church strongly condemned the attacks, saying they violated the constitutional rights to life and freedom of religion and undermined Ethiopia's values of peaceful coexistence. \"Since such an abject act is completely unacceptable under any religious teaching or human rights principles,\" the statement said, the Holy Synod considered the violence an act that \"deserves outright condemnation.\" The Synod called on government institutions to publicly condemn the killings, protect civilians and ensure that those responsible were brought to justice. It urged relevant authorities to uphold the constitutional rights to life and freedom of worship and provide legal protection to prevent similar attacks. The Church also called on security agencies to give the affected communities special attention, provide protection until the security situation is resolved and take preventive measures against further violence. The latest attacks come amid a broader pattern of reported killings and displacement in East Arsi, where Orthodox Christian communities have repeatedly been targeted. In July 2026, the Ethiopian Human Rights Council reported that at least 432 civilians and four local militia members had been killed in targeted attacks across East Arsi since 2021/2022, documenting killings, kidnappings for ransom and forced displacement. Previous attacks in the zone have also involved the destruction and looting of churches and the killing of clergy and worshippers, prompting repeated appeals from religious leaders for investigations and accountability. Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Top Headlines Ethiopia Legal Affairs Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later. The Holy Synod announced that its members would conduct a prayer of absolution ( Tselote Fethat ) on Monday, 26 October, at Menbere Patriarch Kidiste Kidusan Maryam Monastery. It also directed that similar prayers be held in churches and monasteries of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church worldwide on Tuesday, 27 October, following the Divine Liturgy. The Synod further said it had decided to arrange discussions with senior government officials and pursue sustained efforts to resolve the security crisis. Offering condolences to the bereaved families, the Church prayed for the repose of the souls of those killed and called for measures to ensure that its faithful could worship and live without fear of further attacks. Read the original article on Addis Standard . Tagged: Ethiopia East Africa Legal Affairs Copyright © 2026 Addis Standard . All rights reserved. Distributed by AllAfrica Global Media ( allAfrica.com ). To contact the copyright holder directly for corrections — or for permission to republish or make other authorized use of this material, click here. AllAfrica publishes around 600 reports a day from more than 90 news organizations and over 500 other institutions and individuals , representing a diversity of positions on every topic. We publish news and views ranging from vigorous opponents of governments to government publications and spokespersons. Publishers named above each report are responsible for their own content, which AllAfrica does not have the legal right to edit or correct. Articles and commentaries that identify allAfrica.com as the publisher are produced or commissioned by AllAfrica . To address comments or complaints, please Contact us . AllAfrica is a voice of, by and about Africa - aggregating, producing and distributing 600 news and information items daily from over 90 African news organizations and our own reporters to an African and global public. We operate from Cape Town, Dakar, Abuja, Johannesburg, Nairobi and Washington DC. Support our work Sign up for our newsletter For Advertisers Subscribe © 2026 AllAfrica Privacy Policy Contact Us About Us AllAfrica - All the Time Close Sign up for free AllAfrica Newsletters Get the latest in African news delivered straight to your inbox Submit By submitting above, you agree to our privacy policy . Success! Almost finished... We need to confirm your email address. To complete the process, please follow the instructions in the email we just sent you. Error! Error! There was a problem processing your submission. Please try again later.",
      "summary": "The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church's Holy Synod has confirmed the deaths of 60 Orthodox Christians in recent attacks in Oromia's Arsi Zone, occurring on October 20 and 24. The attacks resulted in over 20 injuries and the destruction of 20 homes. Specifically, 14 individuals were killed in Angada Kebele on October 20, while 46 were killed in various locations on October 24. The Holy Synod condemned these acts as \"extremely tragic cruelty,\" highlighting the ongoing violence against Orthodox Christians in the region. They called for government action to protect civilians and ensure justice for the victims. The Synod also urged security agencies to prioritize the safety of affected communities and to implement measures to prevent further violence. This escalation of attacks reflects a broader trend of targeted violence against Orthodox Christians in East Arsi, prompting calls for accountability and investigations into these incidents. The Synod plans to hold prayers for the victims and engage with government officials to address the security crisis.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/holy-synod-confirms-60-orthodox-faithful-killed-in-latest-at-90a7a4893b.html"
    }
  ],
