      "image": "https://softpower.ug/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/HVkUfn5WoAATKRe.jpg",
      "content": "Museveni Urges Somalia to Unite Against Al-Shabaab, Opens Door for Ugandan Coffee Exports - SoftPower News Latest Museveni Urges Somalia to Unite Against Al-Shabaab, Opens Door for Ugandan Coffee Exports 11 minutes ago Museveni Urges Iranian Investors to Build Agro-Processing Factories in Uganda 7 minutes ago Oscar Munanura Promoted to Acting Major General, Appointed Chief of Defence Intelligence and Security 1 hour ago Uganda Bets on Local Cuisines to Grow Tourism as Culinary Training Kicks Off in Mukono 11 hours ago 50 Years of Impact: Top PR Agencies and Communicators Crowned at 10th PRAU Excellence Awards 20 hours ago Uganda Urges Predictable Funding for African Peace Operations 21 hours ago Pearl Bank Wins Inaugural PR Crown Jewel Accolade at 10th PRAU Excellence Awards 21 hours ago Load More Tuesday, October 27, 2026 News Tourism & Travel Business Lifestyle Fashion Regional Kenya Rwanda Tanzania Burundi South Sudan DR Congo Defence & Security Sport Entertainment More Agriculture Africa Columnists Education Health COVID-19 International News News in Pictures OpEd Pearl Of Africa People Politics Special Reports Women No Result View All Result News Tourism & Travel Business Lifestyle Fashion Regional Kenya Rwanda Tanzania Burundi South Sudan DR Congo Defence & Security Sport Entertainment More Agriculture Africa Columnists Education Health COVID-19 International News News in Pictures OpEd Pearl Of Africa People Politics Special Reports Women No Result View All Result No Result View All Result Home News Museveni Urges Somalia to Unite Against Al-Shabaab, Opens Door for Ugandan Coffee Exports by Rogers Atukunda October 27, 2026 President Museveni with Somalia President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud 3 VIEWS President Yoweri Kaguta Museveni has urged Somali leaders and citizens to unite against Al-Shabaab, warning that ideological divisions and a failure to correctly identify the country’s challenges have undermined efforts to achieve lasting peace. President Museveni made the remarks on Monday, October 26, 2026, during a meeting with Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud at State House, Entebbe, where the two leaders also discussed expanding bilateral trade, including direct access for Ugandan coffee to the Somali market. Related Stories Museveni Urges Iranian Investors to Build Agro-Processing Factories in Uganda Uganda Bets on Local Cuisines to Grow Tourism as Culinary Training Kicks Off in Mukono Uganda Urges Predictable Funding for African Peace Operations “The problem of Somalia is ideological and a misdiagnosis of problems,” President Museveni said, drawing parallels with other African countries where divisions have contributed to political instability and exclusion. He called for a united approach to confronting the country’s security challenges, arguing that divisions among the majority could allow a smaller group to maintain influence. “All the problems you’re talking about, we know them. We need to be organised and stand with the main problem,” he said. “Unite the many to defeat the few, but some Africans want to annoy the many to stay with the few,” President Museveni added. The discussions focused on Somalia’s security situation, particularly the fight against Al-Shabaab, as well as strengthening economic cooperation between Kampala and Mogadishu. President Mohamud briefed President Museveni on developments in Somalia and commended the contribution of the Uganda People’s Defence Forces (UPDF) to regional peace and security efforts. Ugandan troops have participated in African Union-led peace support operations in Somalia, supporting Somali security forces in efforts to combat Al-Shabaab and stabilise the country. President Mohamud thanked Uganda for its continued military support and expressed hope that Ugandan troops would remain in Somalia longer to help achieve lasting peace and stability. Al-Shabaab continues to pose a major security threat in Somalia and the wider Horn of Africa, prompting continued cooperation among Somali authorities, African partners and the international community. The Somali leader also thanked Uganda for providing a welcoming environment for Somali nationals living, studying and doing business in the country. Somalia’s Ambassador to Uganda, Fardowsa Mohamed Qanyare, told the meeting that approximately 8,000 Somalis operate businesses in Uganda, while about 15,000 Somali students are enrolled at Ugandan universities. Uganda seeks direct access to Somali coffee market The two presidents also discussed ways to expand bilateral trade, with a particular focus on increasing Ugandan exports to Somalia. President Museveni welcomed the development that Somalia had permitted Uganda to establish a warehouse in the country to market and sell processed Ugandan coffee. The proposed arrangement is expected to give Ugandan exporters a more direct route to Somali consumers, potentially reducing reliance on intermediaries and strengthening commercial links between the two countries. Nelson Tugume, who recently returned from Somalia, told the leaders that the country imports coffee from European markets, with Ugandan coffee reportedly passing through other markets before reaching Somali consumers. The warehouse initiative could help Ugandan exporters access the Somali market more directly while creating opportunities for trade and investment. The discussions underscored efforts by the two governments to complement security cooperation with economic partnerships aimed at strengthening bilateral relations and opening new markets for their products. The meeting was attended by the Minister of State for Foreign Affairs, Haruna Kasolo; the Minister of State for Defence and Veteran Affairs, Huda Abason Oleru; the Permanent Secretary in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Vincent Bagiire; and Uganda’s Ambassador to Somalia, Prof. Sam Tulya-Muhika. Tags: Hassan Sheikh Mohamud Museveni Somalia Uganda Recent Stories Museveni Urges Iranian Investors to Build Agro-Processing Factories in Uganda Museveni Urges Somalia to Unite Against Al-Shabaab, Opens Door for Ugandan Coffee Exports Oscar Munanura Promoted to Acting Major General, Appointed Chief of Defence Intelligence and Security Uganda Bets on Local Cuisines to Grow Tourism as Culinary Training Kicks Off in Mukono 50 Years of Impact: Top PR Agencies and Communicators Crowned at 10th PRAU Excellence Awards Uganda Urges Predictable Funding for African Peace Operations SoftPower News is a subsidiary of SoftPower Communications LLC, a Ugandan digital media group. Keep posted of the latest from Uganda and East Africa. Plot 4B Malcolm X, Kololo P.O Box 1497, Kampala - Uganda Tel: +256-392-001-701 Email: info@softpower.ug This news site is licenced by Uganda Communications Commission (UCC) ADVERTISEMENT News Tourism & Travel Business Lifestyle Regional Defence & Security Sport Entertainment More © SoftPower News Welcome Back! Login to your account below Remember Me Forgotten Password? Retrieve your password Please enter your username or email address to reset your password. Log In Add New Playlist - Select Visibility - Public Private error: Content is protected No Result View All Result News Tourism & Travel Business Lifestyle Fashion Regional Kenya Rwanda Tanzania Burundi South Sudan DR Congo Defence & Security Sport Entertainment More Agriculture Africa Columnists Education Health COVID-19 International News News in Pictures OpEd Pearl Of Africa People Politics Special Reports Women © SoftPower News Not enough quota to unlock this post Unlock left : 0 Buy Quotas Are you sure want to cancel subscription? Yes No",
      "summary": "During a recent meeting in Entebbe, Ugandan President Yoweri Museveni urged Somali leaders and citizens to unite against the militant group Al-Shabaab, emphasizing that ideological divisions have hindered peace efforts in Somalia. Museveni highlighted the need for a collective approach to address security challenges, warning that fragmentation allows a minority to maintain influence over the majority. The discussions also included enhancing bilateral trade, particularly through Ugandan coffee exports to Somalia, with plans for a Ugandan warehouse in Somalia to facilitate direct access to the market. Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud acknowledged Uganda's military support in stabilizing Somalia and expressed gratitude for the welcoming environment for Somali nationals in Uganda. The meeting underscored the importance of combining security cooperation with economic partnerships to strengthen relations between the two nations and open new markets for Ugandan products.",
