      "image": "https://www.dawan.africa/news-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Futfs.io%2Ff%2Fv9D9ylrdiyZgTIP9SzaDxbGRr4v2kWIHzAUPEOe1s6XltBCy",
      "content": "Somalia Could Catch 800,000 Tonnes of Tuna Worth $2 Billion a Year, Fisheries Chief Says | Dawan Africa | Dawan Africa Tuesday, October 27, 2026 Search Home Politics Business Opinion Sports Explainers Cryptocurrency Culture & Society Features News Tech Podcasts Videos Blockchain Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Somalia Kenya Djibouti Ethiopia Eritrea Loading article content... Uncovering the Continent — Through Its Own Lens 21 October Street, 405 Suldan Business Park, Mogadishu, Somalia +252628881171 Info@dawan.africa Quick Links Home Latest News About Us Categories Culture & Society Features Cryptocurrency Tech Blockchain Blockchain Recent Posts Somali Government Denies Reports of Turkish Airstrikes on Army Positions in Leego Oct 27, 2026 Somalia Could Catch 800,000 Tonnes of Tuna Worth $2 Billion a Year, Fisheries Chief Says Oct 27, 2026 Somalia Spent $450.4M on Khat Imports in 2025, More Than Domestic Revenue Oct 27, 2026 Stay Updated with African News Get the latest breaking news and analysis delivered to your inbox. Subscribe Join our community of informed readers. Unsubscribe anytime. © 2026 Dawan Africa. All rights reserved. Privacy Policy Terms of Service Search Somalia Could Catch 800,000 Tonnes of Tuna Worth $2 Billion a Year, Fisheries Chief Says October 27, 2026 2 min read AS By - Reporter Abdullahi Salad MOGADISHU, 27 October 2026 — Somalia could catch up to 800,000 tonnes of tuna a year, worth an estimated $1 billion to $2 billion, the director general of the fisheries ministry, Abdi Dirshe, said on Monday. Speaking at Somalia’s National Fisheries Week, Dirshe said marine resources were the first opportunity investors could take advantage of. He said the 800,000-tonne figure referred to tuna alone and did not include other species. “Somalia is the country with the longest coastline and the most resources in all of Africa,” Dirshe said. He said investors could also make money from fishing boats. Someone who bought 10 to 20 boats could earn millions of dollars, he said, adding that there were also opportunities in building and repairing boats. Dirshe said the country needed large cold storage facilities because fish spoil quickly once they are taken from the sea. Without refrigeration, he said, their quality and safety deteriorate and they can no longer be eaten. More from Somalia Somali Government Denies Reports of Turkish Airstrikes on Army Positions in Leego Somalia Spent $450.4M on Khat Imports in 2025, More Than Domestic Revenue Anyone who built a cold store with a capacity of 1,000 tonnes could make good money, he said, because many people could use it. He added that there were further opportunities in logistics, such as bringing in refrigerated trucks to carry fish from the coast to other areas, and opening large fish shops with their own refrigeration. Fish still make up a small share of Somalia’s exports. According to trade data, fish and other seafood account for about 5% of the country’s goods exports, while live animals make up the largest share. According to Central Bank of Somalia data, livestock accounted for about 73% of goods exports in 2025, worth $923 million, out of total goods exports of $1.26 billion. Discover more Somalia updates Somali Mothers Honoured at Women Leadership Awards in Mogadishu Somalia Says 75 Al-Shabaab Fighters Killed in Bay Region Clash Ad Ad Like ( 0 ) Save ( 0 ) Share More Somalia Stories Back to Top More from Somalia Kenya Senate Gets Digital Tool to Watch County Laws Oct 27, 2026 News Read more → Kenya Flies 50 Top Dairy Cows to Nigeria in Big Trade Step Oct 27, 2026 News Read more → Climate Shocks Exert Pressure on Nyeri County Farmers and Herders Oct 27, 2026 News Read more →",
      "summary": "Somalia has the potential to catch up to 800,000 tonnes of tuna annually, valued between $1 billion and $2 billion, according to Abdi Dirshe, the director general of the fisheries ministry. Speaking during Somalia’s National Fisheries Week, Dirshe emphasized that this figure pertains solely to tuna and does not account for other fish species. He highlighted Somalia's extensive coastline and abundant marine resources as significant opportunities for investors. Dirshe noted that investments in fishing boats and cold storage facilities are crucial, as fish spoil quickly without refrigeration. He suggested that building a cold storage facility with a capacity of 1,000 tonnes could be lucrative. Currently, fish represent only about 5% of Somalia's exports, with livestock dominating at 73%. The fisheries sector presents an untapped market that could enhance the country's economy and create jobs, particularly in logistics and retail.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/somalia-could-catch-800-000-tonnes-of-tuna-worth-2-billion-a-002774a00e.html"
    }
